<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#reichskommissar">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reichskommissar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Reich Commissioner</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A Reichskommissar, or Reich Commissioner, was in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> an official appointed to govern an occupied territory as a civil administrator answerable directly to Hitler. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> Reichskommissariats were set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, Ostland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, headed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josef-terboven">Josef Terboven</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-seyss-inquart">Arthur Seyss-Inquart</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinrich-lohse">Hinrich Lohse</a> and Erich Koch.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title, rendered as Commissioner of the Empire, Reich Commissioner or Imperial Commissioner, was first used in the unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> after 1871 for officials given special tasks, such as the Reichskommissar for emigration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. Adolf Wermuth held it on Heligoland, handed over by the UK on 9 August 1890 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heligoland-zanzibar-treaty">Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty</a>.</p><p>It was also the title of the governors of most of the early colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-nachtigal">Gustav Nachtigal</a> held it in Kamerun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> in July 1884 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> from 7 October 1884, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-von-wissmann">Hermann von Wissmann</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> from 1888 to 1891, and Wilhelm Knappe and Franz Leopold Sonnenschein on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nauru">Nauru</a>. In each case regular governors or other officials later took over.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> revived the title before the war for regions taken back or annexed: Josef Bürckel became Reichskommissar for the return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saar-river">Saar</a> on 1 March 1935 after the plebiscite of 13 January, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-henlein">Konrad Henlein</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a> on 21 October 1938. Bürckel held a similar post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> from 1 May 1939. In each case the office later gave way to that of a Reichsstatthalter.</p><p>In the war the Reichskommissars governed occupied countries as civil administrators with wide authority, and depending on circumstances they could be severely dictatorial and repressive, most notably Erich Koch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. In Norway Terboven proclaimed the deposition of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-vii">Haakon VII</a> on 25 September 1940 and outlawed every party but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasjonal-samling">Nasjonal Samling</a>. Belgium and Northern France received a Reichskommissar, Joseph Grohé, only on 18 July 1944, after four years of military government.</p><p>Alfred Rosenberg had proposed five Reichskommissariats for conquered Soviet lands; only Ostland, set up on 17 July 1941, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, on 20 August 1941, came into being, while Siegfried Kasche and Arno Schickedanz were named for Moskowien and Kaukasien, which the Germans never controlled. The eastern administrations lasted until the Germans were driven out in 1943–44.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reichskommissar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Abushiri Revolt the Reich took over: Hermann Wissmann served as <b>Reichskommissar</b> from 1888 to 1891, and from 1891 imperial governors ruled the colony, from Julius von Soden to the last, Heinrich Schnee, from 1912 to 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>King Haakon VII and the government refused to surrender and went into exile in London, and in April 1940 Hitler appointed Josef Terboven as <b>Reich Commissioner</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/reichskommissariat-norwegen">Reichskommissariat Norwegen</a></li><li><blockquote>Hinrich Lohse became <b>Reich Commissioner</b>, with his seat in Riga.</blockquote><a href="/state/reichskommissariat-ostland">Reichskommissariat Ostland</a></li></ul></section>
